{"name":"Well Blue","hex":"#00888b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"well","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Accurately, competently, satisfactorily.","Completely, fully."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["In good health.","Highly satisfactory"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/well"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Well","blocks":[{"text":"A well is an excavation or structure created on the earth by digging, driving, or drilling to access liquid resources, usually water. The oldest and most common kind of well is a water well, to access groundwater in underground aquifers. The well water is drawn up by a pump, or using containers, such as buckets that are raised mechanically or by hand. Water can also be injected back into the aquifer through the well. Wells were first constructed at least eight thousand years ago and historically vary in construction from a sediment of a dry watercourse to the qanats of Iran, and the stepwells and sakiehs of India. Placing a lining in the well shaft helps create stability, and linings of wood or wickerwork date back at least as far as the Iron Age."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Faryab-_village_dug_well.JPG/330px-Faryab-_village_dug_well.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Well"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Well-cistern.jpg/500px-Well-cistern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Well-cistern.jpg/1280px-Well-cistern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Well-cistern.jpg/500px-Well-cistern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The difference between a well and a cistern is in the source of the water: a cistern collects rainwater whereas a well draws from groundwater.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWell-cistern.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Tunis1960-150_hg.jpg/500px-Tunis1960-150_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Tunis1960-150_hg.jpg/1280px-Tunis1960-150_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Tunis1960-150_hg.jpg/500px-Tunis1960-150_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Camel drawing water from a well, Djerba island, Tunisia, 1960","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATunis1960-150_hg.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/K%C3%BCckhoven_%28Erkelenz%29%2C_Brunnenbaureste_um_5300_v._Ch._.jpg/500px-K%C3%BCckhoven_%28Erkelenz%29%2C_Brunnenbaureste_um_5300_v._Ch._.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/K%C3%BCckhoven_%28Erkelenz%29%2C_Brunnenbaureste_um_5300_v._Ch._.jpg/1280px-K%C3%BCckhoven_%28Erkelenz%29%2C_Brunnenbaureste_um_5300_v._Ch._.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/K%C3%BCckhoven_%28Erkelenz%29%2C_Brunnenbaureste_um_5300_v._Ch._.jpg/500px-K%C3%BCckhoven_%28Erkelenz%29%2C_Brunnenbaureste_um_5300_v._Ch._.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Neolithic Linear Pottery culture well, 5300 BC, Erkelenz, Germany","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AK%C3%BCckhoven_(Erkelenz)%2C_Brunnenbaureste_um_5300_v._Ch._.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Model_well%2C_burial_find%2C_China%2C_Han_dynasty%2C_206_BC_to_220_AD%2C_earthenware_-_%C3%96stasiatiska_museet%2C_Stockholm_-_DSC09578.JPG/500px-Model_well%2C_burial_find%2C_China%2C_Han_dynasty%2C_206_BC_to_220_AD%2C_earthenware_-_%C3%96stasiatiska_museet%2C_Stockholm_-_DSC09578.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Model_well%2C_burial_find%2C_China%2C_Han_dynasty%2C_206_BC_to_220_AD%2C_earthenware_-_%C3%96stasiatiska_museet%2C_Stockholm_-_DSC09578.JPG/1280px-Model_well%2C_burial_find%2C_China%2C_Han_dynasty%2C_206_BC_to_220_AD%2C_earthenware_-_%C3%96stasiatiska_museet%2C_Stockholm_-_DSC09578.JPG?utm_sour